+## Startup
+
+From the repository root, run `python3 service/server.py`. It uses only Python's
+standard library and listens on `127.0.0.1:8765`. Override the port explicitly
+when needed: `PORT=8876 python3 service/server.py`. Keep the process in a terminal
+and use Ctrl-C to stop it. A non-integer PORT or an occupied port prevents startup;
+check stderr and the intended port before retrying.
+
+## Health
+
+In another terminal, probe the same port:
+
+```sh
+curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8765/health
+curl --fail -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8765/accept
+curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8765/health
+```
+
+A fresh process returns `{"status": "ok", "accepted": 0}` from GET /health.
+POST /accept returns HTTP 204 and increments the in-memory counter; the next
+health response contains `accepted: 1`. These transitions were observed in a
+local probe with a temporary PORT. Unknown endpoint paths return 404 according
+to the handler. Connection refusal indicates no reachable listener at that
+address; an HTTP error means a response was received but the request failed.
+This health endpoint checks this process, not a downstream dependency or a
+production deployment.
+
+## Recovery
+
+Stop the process, inspect its stderr/configuration, and restart with the same
+startup command and intended PORT. Restart resets `accepted` to zero: this was
+verified by posting once and restarting a local process. There is no database,
+durable request log or replay mechanism here, so accepted counts cannot be
+recovered from the service after process loss. Escalate unexpected loss to the
+platform on-call engineer; do not describe restart as data recovery.
+
+`docs/operating-status.json` remains `{"status":"unverified"}`. These local
+checks and standards adoption do not authorize deployment; service owners must
+approve operational changes under CONTRIBUTING.md.
